"""Assemble a coherent academic report from accepted project-owned research text."""
import re,json,csv
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];OUT=ROOT/'deliverables/final_submission'
INTRO={
'Introduction':'''Network intrusion research often reports high held-out accuracy without asking whether the evaluation resembles future captures. This project treats data provenance, split design and operating thresholds as part of the scientific problem. It studies the exact MMDS Kaggle release and a separately documented CICIDS2017 mirror, preserving original population counts and the uncertainty of their upstream extraction chains.

The work combines Spark engineering, supervised and novelty models, family rejection, pattern mining, retrospective drift analysis, delayed-label adaptive policies and a validated demonstration application. Its central result is not that a complex detector solves cyberattack detection. It is that numerical correctness, random-split ranking, future operating-point recall and deployable model compatibility are distinct claims that require different evidence.

The report follows that distinction throughout. The source corpus contains16,232,943flows; purged chronological test cohorts, the15,322,987-flow historical replay, the1,997,636-row adaptive primary evaluation and the308,939-row primary external test are not interchangeable denominators. All comparisons retain their declared feature sets, training budgets, calibration rules and label availability.''',
'Problem statement':'''Given a heterogeneous historical flow corpus, construct reproducible numerical layers and evaluate whether detectors trained on earlier or grouped observations maintain useful attack recall at a defensible benign false-positive burden. Extend the analysis to unfamiliar families and external data without assuming that similar column names imply identical measurement semantics.

A second problem is engineering assurance: every displayed result should be traceable to a versioned input, model, transform, threshold and evaluation denominator. A third is computational: measure actual workload actions under a constrained single-machine Spark environment, separating data-volume, thread, storage and cache effects from unsupported multi-node or production claims.''',
'Motivation':'''Correlated duplicate flows can make a row-random split unrealistically easy. Class imbalance can make majority predictions look accurate, while a model with useful ranking can still miss almost every attack at its operational threshold. Marginal distribution alarms can also reflect capture composition rather than a changed decision boundary. These failure modes motivate a study that retains negative findings and unsupported claims instead of selecting only a favorable result.

The academic motivation is to connect algorithms with experimental discipline. The engineering motivation is to provide auditable artifacts and a usable demonstration that computes real predictions rather than replaying static labels. The project does not promise a live network sensor, autonomous defense or a production security service.''',
'Research objectives':'''The objectives are: audit the exact released population; create reusable typed and finite Spark layers; separate grouped-random, chronological and holdout performance; evaluate reference novelty and hybrid policies on matched cohorts; distinguish family-stage diagnostics from upstream binary misses; mine frequent and emerging associations with explicit supports; study historical signals and controlled synthetic changes separately; enforce predict-before-label-release adaptation; reconstruct supported explanations; evaluate conditional common-feature external models; measure bounded scalability workloads; and expose verified archived inference in an isolated application.

Success is defined by reproducible evidence and completed acceptance gates, not a minimum accuracy chosen after observing results. An unsupported feature contract, undefined metric or failed engineering version is recorded explicitly. Public deployment and prospective effectiveness are outside the completed experimental scope.''',
'Literature review and related work':'''Spark supplies a unified processing model and MLlib provides reusable learning pipelines [1,2]. Random forests and gradient boosting are established ensemble methods [3,4]; neither algorithm's reputation guarantees performance on a temporally different flow population. FP-growth supplies a compact frequency-mining strategy [5]. The present project uses these algorithms as reproducible components, not as newly invented methods.

ADWIN and concept-drift literature motivate explicit change-monitoring assumptions [6,7]. Controlled synthetic episode labels allow delay and stationary-alarm evaluation, whereas the historical captures have no authoritative concept-drift ground truth. Precision–recall analysis is relevant under class imbalance [9], but the study reports both ranking and frozen-threshold decisions rather than treating one curve as sufficient.

SHAP gives an additive attribution framework [10]. Here exact interventional linear contributions are validated in log-odds, while unsupported direct tree explanations are omitted. TESSERACT studies spatial and temporal evaluation bias in malware classification [11]; it motivates caution about experiment design but is not an intrusion-detection baseline or a quantitative comparator for these flow results.

CICIDS2017 is documented by its dataset paper and institutional release [8,12]. The accessible Kaggle mirror is version-pinned and audited, while byte identity to an official archive and exact extractor builds remain unverified. Related work therefore informs methodology and terminology; this report makes no state-of-the-art comparison or publication-acceptance claim.''',
'Research gaps':'''The project addresses a reproducibility gap between a favorable model score and the evidence needed to interpret it. The relevant gaps are incomplete cohort accounting, leakage through repeated predictor vectors, unseparated threshold effects, unsupported feature harmonization, retrospective labels described as online learning, attribution without reconstruction checks, and performance claims based on unconsumed lazy actions.

The contribution is an integrated, traceable case study across these concerns. It does not claim to be the first use of Spark, random forests, drift detection or explainable intrusion analysis. The strongest supported novelty is project-level experimental integration and transparent negative-result evidence, not a new learning-theoretic algorithm.''',
'Proposed system':'''The system separates private data engineering, scientific evaluation and public demonstration artifacts. Private Kaggle CPU jobs verify pinned inputs and persist fitted pipelines plus compact aggregate receipts. Independent verification checks numerical reconstructions, calibration, model components and scope counts. Git stores approved aggregates, source/configuration, model identities and bounded portable parameters, not raw private flow records.

The advanced application reads immutable evidence and verifies its own model artifacts before computing manual or bounded CSV predictions. It does not call Kaggle or fit preprocessing on uploaded values. The academic submission reuses those accepted outputs and adds only conceptual architecture diagrams, editorial synthesis and document-quality verification.''',
'System architecture':'''The end-to-end architecture has six boundaries: input provenance, audited numerical representation, experimental role assignment, fitted/scored research components, immutable evidence/model registries and consumer-facing artifacts. Each boundary has an explicit contract. This is important because a correct classifier can still be evaluated on the wrong population, and a correct table can still be mislabeled in a dashboard.

Training-only transforms and frozen operating thresholds constrain information flow. Historical replay preserves archived order without implying live monitoring. Adaptive candidates become active only in a future window after allowed labels and calibration are available. Portable inference preserves original parameters but does not create a new empirical generalization claim.''',
'Experimental results and comparative analysis':'''The master results table is deliberately long-form: each row has a claim identifier, source path, exact selector, metric, denominator and interpretation. It allows comparisons only when the experiment supports them. Source A/B/C random forests share an algorithm family but have different fits and test populations. External E2/E3 comparisons share target rows but use different training domains and frozen calibration. Adaptive primary policies share issued forward observations, while late-period B and synthetic streams remain separate.

The main scientific pattern is a separation between random-split success and difficult future evaluation. The full-cohort Phase4random forest recall falls from approximately82.8%on grouped-random A to about0.2%on chronological B. This does not establish a single causal reason: family exposure, feature distributions, calibration and cohort purging all change. Phase13provides descriptive decomposition and distribution evidence, not causal identification.

Common-feature external evaluation preserves similarly important negatives. Source-only LR detects no primary chronological target attacks. Source RF's target benignFPR exceeds its source-validation ceiling. Target RF improves ranking and fixed-half recall but can sacrifice substantial recall under a stricter selected operating bin. These observations support careful operating-point and compatibility analysis, not a universal model ranking.

Computational evidence is also conditional. Four local threads are not consistently fastest, and Pandas is faster on the bounded matched four-column query. Parquet improves the measured numeric-read/query costs, while cache materialization requires repeated reuse to amortize. Every conclusion is tied to the measured workload, actual row count and stated environment.''',
'Research contributions':'''The first contribution is an auditable data and evaluation chain across the exact16,232,943-flow source release, with predictor-equivalence role separation, training-only transforms and retained denominators. The second is a coherent negative-result account of temporal/family generalization, calibration, novelty, fusion and delayed-label adaptation.

The third contribution is model-level numerical assurance: exact LR logits, centroid distance, hybrid paths, validated linear SHAP and portable LR/RF/K-Means execution have explicit reconstruction or parity checks. The fourth is a matched computational study that forces consumed numeric/model outputs and distinguishes workload scale, threads, format and cache. The fifth is a demonstration-ready research application and an academic package whose figures, tables and claims are content-addressed.

These contributions are supported within this project. There is no claimed new classifier, benchmark superiority, causal attack discovery, prospective live defense validation or multi-node scaling theorem.''',
'Scientific and technical limitations':'''The source mirror's upstream description and extraction revision are incomplete. The external mirror has explicit institutional provenance but no independently authenticated official-byte chain. Both are historical laboratory releases with capture-specific compositions. Exact cross-dataset event identity is not established, and common-vector collisions are not proof of duplicate events.

Repeated flows, finite capture days, group-aware sampling, approximate binned AUC, retrospective label release and bounded enriched cases limit statistical interpretation. Flow rows are not independent replications. Descriptive seed/repetition/capture-deletion ranges are not universal confidence guarantees. The secondary grouped target training budget is larger than an independent10%within-role sample because it shares a keyed hash with role assignment.

Direct interventional tree SHAP and original18-feature external transfer remain unsupported. Novelty is not probability; association is not causation; historical replay is not live monitoring. Single-machine SparkContexts can share a warmed JVM and uncontrolled filesystem cache. Sampled RSS can miss transients, and Pandas process-tree memory can include a resident Spark JVM. Public deployment, authentication/tenant hardening and prospective operational validation remain future work.''',
'Conclusion':'''The completed project demonstrates that large-scale cyberattack research can be made reproducible without hiding weak or negative results. Its most consequential findings are the collapse of some chronological operating points, the mixed value of fusion and adaptation, the difficulty of defensible feature transfer and the workload dependence of computational performance.

The final application computes genuine archived-model predictions and supported explanations while retaining source identities and bounds. Its usability and numerical compatibility are verified separately from detection quality. The final academic package closes the engineering and reporting workflow; it does not convert retrospective evidence into a claim of production readiness.''',
'Future work':'''Future research could authenticate upstream extractor revisions, obtain prospective captures with legitimate ground truth, evaluate a prespecified common-feature transfer model on new untouched data and study calibrated operating policies under realistic analyst costs. Any new model selection would need fresh protocols and independent cohorts.

Engineering extensions could add a separately authorized hosted service, stronger access controls and tenant isolation, carefully monitored model lifecycle management and genuine multi-node benchmarks. These are proposals only. They are not implemented Phase17work or evidence already established by this submission.'''}

def clean(path):
 text=(ROOT/path).read_text();lines=text.splitlines();out=[]
 for i,line in enumerate(lines):
  if i==0 and line.startswith('# '):continue
  if line.startswith('![') or line.startswith('**READY') or line.startswith('**PARTIALLY') or line.startswith('## Z.'):continue
  if line.startswith('#'):
   title=line.lstrip('# ').strip();title=re.sub(r'^[A-Z]\.\s*','',title);line='### '+title
  out.append(line)
 return '\n'.join(out).strip()
def main():
 sections=[]
 def add(title,text,source=None,images=()):
  sections.append(dict(title=title,text=text,source=source,images=list(images)))
 for title in list(INTRO)[:8]:add(title,INTRO[title],images=('end_to_end','evaluation') if title=='System architecture' else ())
 chapters=[('Dataset description and exploratory analysis','reports/dataset_audit.md','data_engineering'),('Distributed data-engineering methodology','reports/phase2_data_engineering.md','leakage_splitting'),('Spark SQL and descriptive traffic analytics','reports/phase3_analytics.md',None),('Supervised attack detection','reports/phase4_supervised_results.md','detection'),('Clustering and novelty detection','reports/phase5_clustering_results.md','phase5_b_distance_histogram'),('Hybrid detection','reports/phase8_hybrid_results.md',None),('Multiclass and open-set classification','reports/phase9_research_report.md',None),('Frequent and emerging pattern mining','reports/phase10_results.md','pattern_mining'),('Historical streaming and drift detection','reports/phase6_streaming_results.md','historical_drift'),('Advanced drift-monitoring evidence','reports/phase11_results.md',None),('Adaptive learning','reports/phase12_results.md','adaptive_lifecycle'),('Explainable AI','reports/phase13_research_report.md','explainable_inference')]
 for title,path,img in chapters:add(title,'This section consolidates the accepted '+title.lower()+' investigation. The original protocol,cohort definitions and limits remain authoritative. No experiment was rerun for this academic report.\n\n'+clean(path),source=path,images=(img,) if img else ())
 text=clean('reports/phase14_research_report.md');cut=text.find('### Volume and worker scalability')
 add('External validation',text[:cut] if cut>=0 else text,source='reports/phase14_research_report.md',images=('external_scalability','phase14_external_models'))
 add('Scalability and execution optimization',text[cut:] if cut>=0 else text,source='reports/phase14_research_report.md',images=('phase14_volume','phase14_workers','phase14_pandas'))
 add('Integrated dashboard and interactive inference',clean('reports/phase15_results.md'),source='reports/phase15_results.md',images=('dashboard',))
 for title in list(INTRO)[8:]:add(title,INTRO[title])
 refs=json.loads((ROOT/'reports/phase16_validation/references.json').read_text());add('References','\n\n'.join('['+str(i)+'] '+r['authors'].replace(' and ', ', ')+'. '+r['title']+'. '+r['venue']+', '+str(r['year'])+'. '+('DOI: '+r['doi'] if r['doi'] else r['url']) for i,r in enumerate(refs,1)))
 add('Appendices and reproducibility instructions','Administrative fields on the title page are editable placeholders. Replace them with approved institutional details;no certificate,signature or approval is fabricated.\n\nSee the accompanying phase completion matrix and master results table for exact hashes and selectors. Reproduce verification from the repository using the established Python3.12.14,Java21,Spark4.3.0environment. Kaggle research archives require the owner-authorized KAGGLE_API_TOKEN secret scoped to www.kaggle.com and api.kaggle.com;never place its value in a document or source file. The deployed application uses compact public parameter artifacts and requires no Kaggle credential.\n\nRun:python -m unittest discover -s tests -v. Launch the separate advanced app with python -m streamlit run dashboard/advanced_app.py --server.maxUploadSize 1. The final reproducibility and deployment guides contain the exact environment paths,checkpoint and private-artifact boundaries. Research reruns are not required for the academic submission.\n\nThe submission inventory and SHA256file bind the documents,figures,tables,screenshots and demonstration fixture. The ZIP excludes credentials,private raw data,caches and full model archives. Publication and public advanced deployment have not been claimed.')
 figures=json.loads((ROOT/'manifests/phase16_figures.json').read_text());lookup={r['name']:r for r in figures if r['path'].endswith('.png')}
 for section in sections:section['images']=[lookup[name] for name in section['images'] if name in lookup]
 content=dict(title='Large-Scale Cyberattack Detection and Evolving Attack Pattern Mining',abstract='This project evaluates leakage-aware cyberattack detection and evolving traffic patterns on an exact16,232,943-flow source release and a2,830,743-flow external mirror. Spark engineering,supervised and novelty models,open-set diagnostics,frequent patterns,historical drift,delayed-label adaptation and explainable inference are assessed with versioned evidence. Strong grouped-random results coexist with weak chronological recall;hybrid/adaptive policies show mixed trade-offs. External evaluation uses three conditionally compatible predictors and preserves source-only versus target-trained distinctions. Forty-eight volume/worker measurements plus16warmups and130optimization measurements show workload-dependent performance;at1Mmatched rows Pandas takes0.242s versusSpark0.616s for the measured query. A separate dashboard executes archived LR,RF and K-Means with2,445Spark parity probes. The contribution is an auditable experimental and software chain,not a production detector or state-of-the-art claim.',keywords='Apache Spark;intrusion detection;temporal generalization;concept drift;adaptive learning;explainability;reproducibility',sections=sections)
 # Editorial spacing in the newly authored synthesis only; archive identifiers remain exact.
 for section in sections:
  if section['source'] is None and section['title']!='References':
   section['text']=re.sub(r'(?<=[a-z])(?=\d)', ' ', section['text'])
   section['text']=re.sub(r'(?<=\d)(?=[a-z])', ' ', section['text'])
 content['abstract']='This project evaluates leakage-aware cyberattack detection and evolving traffic patterns on a 16,232,943-flow source release and a 2,830,743-flow external mirror. Spark engineering, supervised and novelty models, open-set diagnostics, frequent patterns, historical drift, delayed-label adaptation and explainable inference are assessed with versioned evidence. Strong grouped-random results coexist with weak chronological recall; hybrid and adaptive policies show mixed trade-offs. External evaluation uses three conditionally compatible predictors and preserves source-only versus target-trained distinctions. Forty-eight volume/worker measurements, 16 warm-ups and 130 optimization measurements show workload-dependent performance: at one million matched rows, Pandas takes 0.242 seconds versus Spark 0.616 seconds for the measured query. A separate dashboard executes archived LR, RF and K-Means with 2,445 Spark parity probes. The contribution is an auditable experimental and software chain, not a production detector or a state-of-the-art claim.'
 sections[-1]['text']+='\n\nMathematical conventions: recall = TP/(TP+FN); benign FPR = FP/(FP+TN); precision = TP/(TP+FP). Undefined denominators remain undefined. A frozen threshold tau produces an alert when score(x) >= tau. Logistic probability is 1/(1+exp(-z)); additive linear SHAP reconstructs z, not the probability. K-Means novelty uses the minimum squared Euclidean distance to an archived center after its training-only transform. FP-growth support is count(A)/N and confidence(A => B) is count(A union B)/count(A). These definitions do not make associations causal or novelty scores probabilities.'
 (OUT/'sources/report_content.json').write_text(json.dumps(content,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
 md=['# '+content['title'],'\nStudent:[editable]\n\nRegistration:[editable]\n\nSupervisor:[editable]\n\nInstitution/programme/year:[editable]','\n## Abstract\n'+content['abstract'],'\nKeywords:'+content['keywords'],'\n## Table of contents\n'+'\n'.join(str(i)+'. '+s['title'] for i,s in enumerate(sections,1))]
 for i,s in enumerate(sections,1):
  md+=['\n## '+str(i)+'. '+s['title'],s['text']]
  if s['source']:md+=['\nEvidence source:'+s['source']]
  for fig in s['images']:md+=['\n!['+fig['name'].replace('_',' ')+'](../figures/'+Path(fig['path']).name+')']
 (OUT/'sources/project_report.md').write_text('\n\n'.join(md)+'\n');print('Report source',sum(len(s['text'].split()) for s in sections),'words;',len(sections),'body sections')
if __name__=='__main__':main()
